title: "GPIE — Green Policy Intelligence Engine: Independently Verifying Environmental Policy Claims Using Satellite Data"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GPIE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/GPIE"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21756661"
    description: "Zenodo archive of GPIE v1.2.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21756661"
abstract: >-
  GPIE is a geospatial causal-inference framework that tests whether the European
  Climate Law (Regulation (EU) 2021/1119, adopted 30 June 2021, in force 29 July
  2021) produced a
  statistically distinguishable reduction in tropospheric NO2 pollution across the
  EU-27, using Sentinel-5P TROPOMI satellite observations (2019-2024) and a
  Difference-in-Differences design with a 9-country non-EU comparison group
  (United Kingdom, Norway, Switzerland, Iceland, Albania, Bosnia and Herzegovina,
  Montenegro, North Macedonia, Serbia). With NO2 aggregated to true monthly means,
  EU-27 NO2 fell relative to the comparison group (pooled DiD -1.52e-6 mol/m2,
  about 4.9% of the pre-treatment EU mean, p = 0.013, cluster-robust), mainly in
  the 14 higher-baseline member states. The design cannot attribute this to the
  Climate Law: every alternative cutoff date is significant, an EU-specific
  linear trend absorbs the treatment-date term, and the estimate weakens without
  the winter months, with area weights and with log GDP. On a 0.1 degree grid the
  decline is regional, not institutional: polluted pixels lost 15-25% in
  north-western Europe, including the non-EU United Kingdom and Switzerland, and
  0-6% in south-eastern Europe, including EU members. Matching EU and comparison
  pixels on pre-treatment level cuts the estimate to -0.21e-6 (p = 0.341). A
  secondary NDVI outcome shows a relative decline (p = 0.005), reported as an
  exploratory association. Earlier versions, based on end-of-month NO2 snapshots,
  reported a pooled null and are superseded.
keywords:
  - causal inference
  - difference-in-differences
  - synthetic control
  - spatial autocorrelation
  - treatment effect heterogeneity
  - satellite remote sensing
  - Sentinel-5P
  - air pollution
  - NO2
  - NDVI
  - European Green Deal
  - policy evaluation
  - geospatial analysis
